<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-laws-of-probability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Laws of Probability</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.2 Probability II</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>Apply laws of probability to predict the likelihood of events</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Learners discover that simply adding <m>P(A)</m> and <m>P(B)</m> can overcount outcomes in the overlap, motivating the general addition rule and the idea of conditional probability.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    After part (c), ask: <q>Why did adding <m>P(A) + P(B)</m> give the wrong answer?</q> Push learners to identify the double-counted outcomes. The correction in part (d) should feel natural — <q>we added the overlap twice, so we subtract it once.</q>
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-cards-addition-rule">
        <title>Cards and Overlapping Events</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A card is drawn at random from a set of 10 cards numbered 1 to 10. Define:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>A = <q>card is even</q> = <m>\{2, 4, 6, 8, 10\}</m>,</p></li>
                    <li><p>B = <q>card shows a number greater than 6</q> = <m>\{7, 8, 9, 10\}</m>.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            List all outcomes in <m>A \cup B</m> (cards that are even <em>or</em> greater than 6, or both). How many outcomes are there? Hence find <m>P(A \cup B)</m>.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>A \cup B = \{2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10\}</m> — 7 outcomes. <m>P(A \cup B) = \frac{7}{10}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Calculate <m>P(A) + P(B)</m>. Is this the same as <m>P(A \cup B)</m>?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>P(A) + P(B) = \frac{5}{10} + \frac{4}{10} = \frac{9}{10} \neq \frac{7}{10}</m>. No — it is too large.
                                Ask: <q>Which outcomes were counted twice in <m>P(A) + P(B)</m>?</q>
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            List the outcomes in <m>A \cap B</m> (cards that are both even <em>and</em> greater than 6). Which outcomes did you count twice in part (b)?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>A \cap B = \{8, 10\}</m>. These two outcomes appear in both A and B, so they were counted twice.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Show that <m>P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B) = P(A \cup B)</m>.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>\frac{5}{10} + \frac{4}{10} - \frac{2}{10} = \frac{7}{10} = P(A \cup B)</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Given that the card drawn is known to be greater than 6 (i.e. the card is in B), how many cards from B are also even? Hence estimate the probability that a card from B is even.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                B = {7,8,9,10}. Of these, {8,10} are even — 2 out of 4. So the probability is <m>\frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2}</m>. This is called the <term>conditional probability</term> of A given B, written <m>P(A \mid B)</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To state and name the four main laws of probability, connecting each to the activity.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Name the rules in order: (1) complement rule — already known from Grade 10; (2) general addition rule — just discovered in the activity; (3) multiplication rule for independent events — from the previous section; (4) general multiplication rule using conditional probability — motivated by part (e) of the activity.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Write <m>P(A \mid B) = \dfrac{P(A \cap B)}{P(B)}</m> on the board. Confirm with the activity: <m>P(A \mid B) = \frac{2/10}{4/10} = \frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2}</m> matches what learners found by listing.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Laws of Probability</title>
        <p>
            Let A and B be events in a sample space S with <m>P(B) \gt 0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Complement Rule</title>
                    <p>
                        <md>P(A') = 1 - P(A).</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>General Addition Rule</title>
                    <p>
                        <md>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B).</md>
                        When A and B are mutually exclusive, <m>P(A \cap B) = 0</m> and this reduces to <m>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Conditional Probability</title>
                    <p>
                        The probability of A <em>given that</em> B has occurred is
                        <md>P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(A \cap B)}{P(B)}.</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>General Multiplication Rule</title>
                    <p>
                        Rearranging the conditional probability formula:
                        <md>P(A \cap B) = P(B) \times P(A \mid B) = P(A) \times P(B \mid A).</md>
                        When A and B are independent, <m>P(A \mid B) = P(A)</m> and this reduces to <m>P(A \cap B) = P(A) \times P(B)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Applying the Addition Rule and Complement</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                For two events A and B, <m>P(A) = 0.4</m>, <m>P(B) = 0.35</m>, and <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.15</m>. Find:
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p><m>P(A \cup B)</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>P((A \cup B)')</m> — the probability that neither A nor B occurs.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B) = 0.4 + 0.35 - 0.15 = 0.60.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P((A \cup B)') = 1 - P(A \cup B) = 1 - 0.60 = 0.40.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Conditional Probability</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In a class of 40 learners, 25 study Mathematics, 20 study Sciences, and 10 study both.
                One learner is selected at random.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(\text{Maths})</m>, <m>P(\text{Sciences})</m>, and <m>P(\text{Maths} \cap \text{Sciences})</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Given that the selected learner studies Sciences, find the probability that they also study Mathematics.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Are studying Mathematics and studying Sciences independent events?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(M) = \frac{25}{40} = \frac{5}{8}</m>, <m>P(S) = \frac{20}{40} = \frac{1}{2}</m>, <m>P(M \cap S) = \frac{10}{40} = \frac{1}{4}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Using the conditional probability formula:
                            <md>P(M \mid S) = \frac{P(M \cap S)}{P(S)} = \frac{1/4}{1/2} = \frac{1}{2}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Check: <m>P(M) \times P(S) = \frac{5}{8} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{5}{16}</m>. But <m>P(M \cap S) = \frac{1}{4} = \frac{4}{16} \neq \frac{5}{16}</m>. The events are <term>not independent</term>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>General Multiplication Rule (Without Replacement)</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A bag contains 5 red marbles and 3 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn one after the other <em>without replacement</em>. Find the probability that both marbles are red.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Let <m>R_1</m> = <q>first marble is red</q> and <m>R_2</m> = <q>second marble is red</q>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>P(R_1) = \frac{5}{8}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Given the first was red, 4 red and 3 blue remain (7 total), so <m>P(R_2 \mid R_1) = \frac{4}{7}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                By the general multiplication rule:
                <md>P(R_1 \cap R_2) = P(R_1) \times P(R_2 \mid R_1) = \frac{5}{8} \times \frac{4}{7} = \frac{20}{56} = \frac{5}{14}.</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                This matches the result from Section <xref ref="sec-theoretical-probability" text="title"/>, where we found <m>P(\text{both red}) = \frac{{}^5C_2}{{}^8C_2} = \frac{10}{28} = \frac{5}{14}</m> by counting. The multiplication rule and the combinations approach give the same answer. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Finding a Probability Using Multiple Laws</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In a group of students, <m>P(A) = 0.6</m> (probability of passing an English test) and <m>P(B) = 0.7</m> (probability of passing a Swahili test). The two tests are taken independently.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the probability of passing both tests.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the probability of passing at least one test.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the probability of passing exactly one test.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Since the tests are independent, <m>P(A \cap B) = P(A) \times P(B) = 0.6 \times 0.7 = 0.42</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.42</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(A \cup B) = 0.6 + 0.7 - 0.42 = 0.88.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Exactly one test means passing A but not B, or passing B but not A.
                            <md>P(\text{exactly one}) = P(A \cup B) - P(A \cap B) = 0.88 - 0.42 = 0.46.</md>
                            Alternatively: <m>P(A) \times P(B') + P(A') \times P(B) = 0.6 \times 0.3 + 0.4 \times 0.7 = 0.18 + 0.28 = 0.46</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For two events A and B, <m>P(A) = 0.3</m>, <m>P(B) = 0.5</m>, and <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.1</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(A \cup B)</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P((A \cup B)')</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(A \cup B) = 0.3 + 0.5 - 0.1 = 0.7</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P((A \cup B)') = 1 - 0.7 = 0.3</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The probability that a machine produces a defective item is 0.12. Find the probability that a randomly chosen item is <em>not</em> defective.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>P(\text{not defective}) = 1 - 0.12 = 0.88</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In a school of 50 learners, 30 play football, 20 play basketball, and 8 play both. A learner is selected at random.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Given that the learner plays football, find the probability they also play basketball.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Given that the learner plays basketball, find the probability they also play football.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(\text{basketball} \mid \text{football}) = \dfrac{8/50}{30/50} = \dfrac{8}{30} = \dfrac{4}{15}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(\text{football} \mid \text{basketball}) = \dfrac{8/50}{20/50} = \dfrac{8}{20} = \dfrac{2}{5}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A drawer contains 4 black pens and 2 white pens. Two pens are chosen one after the other without replacement. Find the probability that both pens are black.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>P(\text{both black}) = \dfrac{4}{6} \times \dfrac{3}{5} = \dfrac{12}{30} = \dfrac{2}{5}</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Three events A, B, C satisfy:
                    <md>P(A) = 0.5,\quad P(B) = 0.4,\quad P(C) = ?,</md>
                    <md>P(A \cap B) = 0.20,\quad P(A \cap C) = 0.15,\quad P(B \cap C) = 0.12,\quad P(A \cap B \cap C) = 0.06,</md>
                    and <m>P(A \cup B \cup C) = 0.75</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Using the inclusion-exclusion formula
                    <md>P(A \cup B \cup C) = P(A) + P(B) + P(C) - P(A \cap B) - P(A \cap C) - P(B \cap C) + P(A \cap B \cap C),</md>
                    find <m>P(C)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <md>0.75 = 0.5 + 0.4 + P(C) - 0.20 - 0.15 - 0.12 + 0.06.</md>
                    <md>0.75 = 0.49 + P(C) \implies P(C) = 0.26.</md>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

